arXiv:2509.08257v1 [cs.RO] 10 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yongkai Tian
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>State Key Laboratory of Complex &amp; Critical Software Environment, Beihang University, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yirong Qi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>State Key Laboratory of Complex &amp; Critical Software Environment, Beihang University, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xin Yu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>State Key Laboratory of Complex &amp; Critical Software Environment, Beihang University, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wenjun Wu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>State Key Laboratory of Complex &amp; Critical Software Environment, Beihang University, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jie Luo
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>*Corresponding Author: Jie Luo <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">luojie@buaa.edu.cn.</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>State Key Laboratory of Complex &amp; Critical Software Environment, Beihang University, Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In robotic systems, the performance of reinforcement learning depends on the rationality of predefined reward functions. However, manually designed reward functions often lead to policy failures due to inaccuracies. Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) addresses this problem by inferring implicit reward functions from expert demonstrations. Nevertheless, existing methods rely heavily on large amounts of expert demonstrations to accurately recover the reward function. The high cost of collecting expert demonstrations in robotic applications, particularly in multi-robot systems, severely hinders the practical deployment of IRL. Consequently, improving sample efficiency has emerged as a critical challenge in multi-agent inverse reinforcement learning (MIRL). Inspired by the symmetry inherent in multi-agent systems, this work theoretically demonstrates that leveraging symmetry enables the recovery of more accurate reward functions. Building upon this insight, we propose a universal framework that integrates symmetry into existing multi-agent adversarial IRL algorithms, thereby significantly enhancing sample efficiency. Experimental results from multiple challenging tasks have demonstrated the effectiveness of this framework. Further validation in physical multi-robot systems has shown the practicality of our method.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) has made notable achievements in multi-robot systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The efficacy of MARL primarily arises from its ability to optimize manually designed reward functions. However, this also highlights a critical challenge: devising suitable reward functions for complex and poorly defined tasks is often impractical <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, improperly designed reward functions may result in undesirable agent behavior. To mitigate these problems, Multi-Agent Imitation Learning has emerged as a prevalent algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, capable of directly learning the policy from expert demonstrations, thus avoiding the need for a detailed reward function design.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08257v1/symmetry.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/256;" width="476" height="256"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Multi-agent inverse reinforcement learning with symmetry. When the global state undergoes rotation, the agents’ actions correspondingly rotate. This property can be utilized to recover a more accurate feasible reward set.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the field of imitation learning, two predominant methodologies are employed: Behavior Cloning (BC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. BC leverages supervised learning to derive policies from expert state-action pairs. Although BC has achieved success in some simple scenarios, it suffers from compounded errors caused by the covariate shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. On the other hand, IRL recovers the implicit reward function from expert demonstrations and then uses this reward function to train the policy, allowing it to be applied to more complex tasks. However, one significant challenge it faces is the need for large-scale and diverse expert demonstrations, leading to sample inefficiency problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. In multi-robot systems, due to the increased complexity of the system, the higher cost of collecting expert demonstrations and the need for more expert demonstrations to train the model make the problem of sample inefficiency even more pronounced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Addressing the challenge of sample inefficiency in Multi-Agent IRL (MIRL) fundamentally involves reducing the reliance on expert demonstrations and recovering more accurate reward functions from fewer samples. Utilizing prior knowledge is an effective way that allows neural networks to learn more general knowledge from limited datasets, thereby significantly improving sample efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Multi-agent systems (MAS) commonly exhibit properties known as symmetry, including equivariance and invariance. For example, in Pursuit task depicted in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, when the environment state rotates, the agent’s actions rotate accordingly, demonstrating equivariance. In contrast, metrics such as the distance between agents remain unchanged, exhibiting invariance. Symmetry not only serves as a fundamental property of MAS but also plays a crucial role in multi-agent learning. However, its application in MIRL has not been widely explored. While symmetry has been demonstrated to improve algorithmic performance in the context of MARL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, its theoretical basis does not apply to MIRL.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present the first proof that leveraging the symmetry of MAS can recover more accurate reward functions, bridging the theoretical gap in the performance improvement of MIRL using symmetry. Based on this theory, we propose a novel Symmetry-Guided Framework (SGF) for multi-agent adversarial IRL algorithms, aimed at improving the sample efficiency. This framework comprises two primary modules: a Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter (SGDA) and a Symmetry-Aware Discriminator (SAD). The SGDA performs geometric transformations on expert and generated demonstrations to expand the dataset. The SAD is used to enhance the ability to recognize symmetric data. The key contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide theoretical proof that leveraging symmetry can recover better reward functions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a Symmetry-Guided Framework for multi-agent adversarial IRL, including the Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter and Symmetry-Aware Discriminator.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our method across multiple multi-agent tasks, demonstrating its ability to learn effective policies from fewer expert demonstrations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We deploy our method in a physical multi-robot environment, confirming its effectiveness in the real world.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Agent Adversarial Inverse Reinforcement Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-agent generative adversarial imitation learning (MA-GAIL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> employs a mini-max objective to train agents by alternating between optimizing discriminators <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\ldots,D_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\ldots,D_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and policies <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. These discriminators discern between agent-generated and expert-generated state-action pairs, with agent policies refined via MARL using rewards derived from the discriminators. Multi-agent Adversarial Inverse Reinforcement Learning (MA-AIRL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> modifies the discriminators inspired by the logistic stochastic best response equilibrium (LSBRE). Additionally, MA-AIRL defines reward functions as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log D_{i}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})-\log(1-D_{i}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log D_{i}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})-\log(1-D_{i}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively capturing the reward dynamics leading to expert behaviors. Numerous subsequent works are proposed based on these two algorithms. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> applies an attention mechanism to the generator to enhance the scalability of MA-GAIL. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> extends AIRL to a multi-agent framework using parameter sharing and explores its application in the biological domain. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> adapts AIRL to the distributed domain.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symmetry in Multi-Agent Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have validated the effectiveness of applying symmetry in MARL. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> utilized rotational symmetry for data augmentation and symmetric consistency loss to improve sample efficiency in MARL. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> extended <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to include scenarios of imperfect symmetry. Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> introduced an adaptive data augmentation method to boost sample efficiency. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> developed policy networks that ensure permutation invariance, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> designed policy networks with rotational symmetry. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> leveraged the hierarchical symmetry of MAS to improve algorithm performance. Although the effectiveness of symmetry has been validated in MARL, its theory does not apply to MIRL. Only <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> incorporates symmetry into the GAN framework similar to ours; however, it is not a MIRL algorithm. The application of symmetry in MIRL has not been deeply explored. This paper is the first to leverage symmetry to improve the sample efficiency of MIRL algorithms and provides theoretical proof for this method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivariance and Invariance</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In MAS, symmetry often appears as equivariance and invariance to transformations. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}:X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}:X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> represent a transformation corresponding to an element <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of some abstract group <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. A function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits equivariance to the transformation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a related transformation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g}:Y\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g}:Y\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> in the output space that satisfies:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(L_{g}(\mathbf{x}))=K_{g}(f(\mathbf{x}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(L_{g}(\mathbf{x}))=K_{g}(f(\mathbf{x})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is considered invariant for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> if it meets the following condition:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(L_{g}(\mathbf{x}))=f(\mathbf{x})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(L_{g}(\mathbf{x}))=f(\mathbf{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">indicating that the output of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> does not change despite applying <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Markov Game</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we model the MAS as a Markov Game <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> without reward (MG\R), which is defined by the tuple <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{S},\{\mathcal{A}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N},P,\gamma,\bm{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{S},\{\mathcal{A}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N},P,\gamma,\bm{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of agents and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the state space. Each agent <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> has an associated action space <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the joint action space being the Cartesian product <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{A}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{A}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The state transition function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> defines the probability distribution over the next state given the current state and joint action. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}(\bm{a}|s)=\prod_{i=1}^{N}\pi_{i}(a_{i}|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}(\bm{a}|s)=\prod_{i=1}^{N}\pi_{i}(a_{i}|s)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the joint policy, where we use bold variables without subscript <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the concatenation of all agents (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the joint policy and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the actions of all agents). Given an MG\R <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and a reward function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\cup r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\cup r</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the MG obtained by pairing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Definition</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid reliance on a specific MIRL algorithm, we discuss the set of all feasible reward functions for the expert’s policy. The MIRL problem is defined by a tuple <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=(\mathcal{M},\bm{\pi}_{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=(\mathcal{M},\bm{\pi}_{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an MG\R and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is an expert joint policy. Essentially, the MIRL problem involves recovering a reward function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the expert joint policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimal for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\cup r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\cup r</annotation></semantics></math>. Typically, the reward function that satisfies this condition is not unique. The collection of all such reward functions that meet this criterion is referred to as the feasible reward set, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the MIRL problem <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, the feasible reward set for the single-agent IRL problem is characterized. Here, we extend analogous findings to the multi-agent context.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Feasible Reward Set)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A reward function <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible for <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(s,\bm{a})=-\zeta\mathbb{I}_{\{\bm{\pi}_{E}(\bm{a}|s)=0\}}+V(s)-\gamma\sum\nolimits_{s^{\prime}}P(s^{\prime}|s,\bm{a})V(s^{\prime})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝝅</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(s,\bm{a})=-\zeta\mathbb{I}_{\{\bm{\pi}_{E}(\bm{a}|s)=0\}}+V(s)-\gamma\sum\nolimits_{s^{\prime}}P(s^{\prime}|s,\bm{a})V(s^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the indicator function, which takes the value 1 when <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{E}(\bm{a}|s)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝛑</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{E}(\bm{a}|s)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and 0 otherwise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, the first term depends only on the expert joint policy <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and does not depend on the MG. The other parameter can be interpreted as reward-shaping through the value function <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Error Propagation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we study the error propagation. In practice, IRL is trained using a transition tuples dataset <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}=\{(s_{j},\bm{a}_{j},s^{\prime}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}=\{(s_{j},\bm{a}_{j},s^{\prime}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> collected by the expert policy, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{\prime}_{j}\sim P(\cdot|s_{j},\bm{a}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}_{j}\sim P(\cdot|s_{j},\bm{a}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{a}_{j}\sim\bm{\pi}_{E}(\cdot|s_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{j}\sim\bm{\pi}_{E}(\cdot|s_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the dataset size. Given <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can get the empirical transition model <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and the empirical expert joint policy <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby obtaining an approximate version <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})</annotation></semantics></math> of the MIRL problem <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the empirical MG\R. The feasible reward set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained through <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially an estimate of the true feasible reward set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is inevitably some error between them. We extend the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> to the multi-agent context.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Error Propagation)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given two MIRL problems <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=(\mathcal{M},\bm{\pi}_{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛑</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=(\mathcal{M},\bm{\pi}_{E})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}\in\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}\in\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|r(s,\bm{a})-\hat{r}(s,\bm{a})|\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|r(s,\bm{a})-\hat{r}(s,\bm{a})|\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\zeta\mathbb{I}_{\{\bm{\pi}_{E}(a|s)=0\}}\mathbb{I}_{\{\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}(a|s)&gt;0\}}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝝅</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\zeta\mathbb{I}_{\{\bm{\pi}_{E}(a|s)=0\}}\mathbb{I}_{\{\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}(a|s)&gt;0\}}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma\sum_{s^{\prime}}|V(s^{\prime})(P(s^{\prime}|s,a)-\hat{P}(s^{\prime}|s,a))|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma\sum_{s^{\prime}}|V(s^{\prime})(P(s^{\prime}|s,a)-\hat{P}(s^{\prime}|s,a))|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symmetry-Guided MIRL</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to design an algorithm that leverages the symmetry of MAS to improve the sample efficiency of MIRL algorithms and recover more accurate reward functions. The symmetry of MAS can be modeled through MG. Based on MG\R, we propose the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-invariant Markov Game without reward</span> (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R) for MIRL.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<math id="Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An MG\R is said to be <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant if <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, it satisfies the following equations:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{i}(a_{i}|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{i}(a_{i}|s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\pi_{i}(K_{g}[a_{i}]|L_{g}[s])," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\pi_{i}(K_{g}[a_{i}]|L_{g}[s]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(s^{\prime}|s,\bm{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(s^{\prime}|s,\bm{a})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(L_{g}[s^{\prime}]|L_{g}[s],K_{g}[\bm{a}])," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(L_{g}[s^{\prime}]|L_{g}[s],K_{g}[\bm{a}]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the transformations in state space and action space, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This paper aims to improve sample efficiency by designing algorithms based on the properties of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R. The equivalent perspective of recovering the reward function from expert demonstrations <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}=\{(s_{j},\bm{a}_{j},s^{\prime}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}=\{(s_{j},\bm{a}_{j},s^{\prime}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> based on <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R is to recover the reward function from an augmented expert demonstration <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau_{E}^{\mathcal{G}}=\{(gs_{j},g\bm{a},gs^{\prime}_{j})|g\in\mathcal{G},(s_{j},\bm{a}_{j},s_{j})\in\tau_{E}\}_{j=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}^{\mathcal{G}}=\{(gs_{j},g\bm{a},gs^{\prime}_{j})|g\in\mathcal{G},(s_{j},\bm{a}_{j},s_{j})\in\tau_{E}\}_{j=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we can formally define the Symmetry-Guided MIRL problem as follow.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Symmetry-Guided MIRL Problem)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an empirical MIRL problem <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, a Symmetry-Guided MIRL problem is a pair <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}}^{\mathcal{G}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}^{\mathcal{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}}^{\mathcal{G}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}^{\mathcal{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{M}}^{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{M}}^{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}^{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}^{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical expert joint policy, which are formed from the augmented expert demonstrations <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}^{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}^{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The recovered reward set through <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, by combining Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Error Propagation) ‣ IV-B Error Propagation ‣ IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Symmetry-Guided MIRL Problem) ‣ IV-C Symmetry-Guided MIRL ‣ IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we can conclude that the Symmetry-Guided MIRL problem can recover a more accurate reward function, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We formally state this as the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Symmetry-Guided MIRL Improvement)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the empirical MIRL problem <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{B}^{\mathcal{G}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}}^{\mathcal{G}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}^{\mathcal{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{B}^{\mathcal{G}}=(\hat{\mathcal{M}}^{\mathcal{G}},\hat{\bm{\pi}}_{E}^{\mathcal{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the upper bound of error propagation satisfies the following relation:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|r(s,\bm{a})-\hat{r}(s,\bm{a})|^{U}\geq|r(s,\bm{a})-\hat{r}^{\mathcal{G}}(s,\bm{a})|^{U}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|r(s,\bm{a})-\hat{r}(s,\bm{a})|^{U}\geq|r(s,\bm{a})-\hat{r}^{\mathcal{G}}(s,\bm{a})|^{U},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}^{\mathcal{G}}\in\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}^{\mathcal{G}}\in\mathcal{R}_{\hat{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> denotes upper bound.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Symmetry-Guided MIRL Improvement) ‣ IV-C Symmetry-Guided MIRL ‣ IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we can conclude that the error propagation with symmetry guidance in the worst case is always no greater than the error propagation in the worst case without symmetry guidance. In other words, Symmetry-Guided MIRL corresponds to a more accurate feasible reward set. All proofs are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08257v1/framework.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/255;" width="476" height="255"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the proposed Symmetry-Guided Framework (SGF). The framework consists of two main modules. The Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter performs geometric transformations to augment the given data. The Symmetry-Aware Discriminator is used to enhance the ability to identify symmetric data. The subscript <math id="S4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> denotes entities related to the generator, while the subscript <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> denotes entities related to the expert demonstrations.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Symmetry-Guided Framework</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, we propose a Symmetry-Guided Framework (SGF) for multi-agent adversarial IRL to improve the sample efficiency. It is worth noting that the theory presented in this paper is not limited to these algorithms and can also be applied to other MIRL algorithms. The SGF consists of two primary components: Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter (SGDA) and Symmetry-Aware Discriminator (SAD). The overall architecture is depicted in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-C Symmetry-Guided MIRL ‣ IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the training process is shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ V-A2 Action Transformation ‣ V-A Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter ‣ V Symmetry-Guided Framework ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Symmetry-Guided MIRL Problem) ‣ IV-C Symmetry-Guided MIRL ‣ IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the most direct way to leverage symmetry in MIRL is through data augmentation. To overcome the limitation of insufficient expert demonstrations in multi-agent adversarial IRL, this paper proposes a Symmetry-Guided Demonstration Augmenter that utilizes the symmetry in multi-robot systems to augment the dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of multi-robot systems, symmetry is primarily manifested through equivariance and invariance with respect to rotations and reflections. These geometric transformations are represented by the orthogonal group <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n)=\{Z\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}:Z^{T}Z=I\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n)=\{Z\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}:Z^{T}Z=I\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose of matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> represents the identity matrix. In this paper, we focus on a discrete subgroup of the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> group, specifically the dihedral group <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}=\{R,S\mid R^{n}=I,S^{2}=I,SRS=R^{-1}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}=\{R,S\mid R^{n}=I,S^{2}=I,SRS=R^{-1}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> signify rotation and reflection operations, respectively. The <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> group includes <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> elements, consisting of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rotational and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> reflective transformations. In the subsequent section, we explore the application of data augmentation based on the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> group within the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span>1 </span>State Transformation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-robot systems, the environmental features can be of various types. Some features exhibit equivariance to geometric transformations, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{equ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{equ}</annotation></semantics></math>, while others show invariance, remaining unaffected by these transformations, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{inv}</annotation></semantics></math>. We represent the environmental state as the concatenation of these two types of features, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=[s_{equ},s_{inv}]\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[s_{equ},s_{inv}]\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{equ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{equ}</annotation></semantics></math>, our module applies predefined geometric transformations <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to simulate potential real-world changes in orientation and position. For example, features representing robot coordinates are rotated across various angles to generate new feasible states. On the other hand, since <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{inv}</annotation></semantics></math> is unaffected by geometric transformations, it is preserved in its original form during data augmentation. Invariance typically applies to features such as the internal states of robots and distances. In general, the impact of the geometric transformations <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}[s]=[L_{g}[s_{equ}],s_{inv}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}[s]=[L_{g}[s_{equ}],s_{inv}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span>2 </span>Action Transformation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant MG\R, when geometric transformations act on the environmental state, the agents’ actions are also subjected to corresponding transformations. Similar to the representation of environmental states, the agents’ actions are categorized into equivariant actions <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{equ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{equ}</annotation></semantics></math> and invariant actions <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{inv}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}=[\bm{a}_{equ},\bm{a}_{inv}]\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}=[\bm{a}_{equ},\bm{a}_{inv}]\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{equ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{equ}</annotation></semantics></math>, typically directional actions, the same geometric transformations <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the state are applied to ensure consistency between the state and the action. During augmentation, invariant actions <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{inv}</annotation></semantics></math> are treated as constants since their execution is independent of the geometric properties of the environment. Therefore, the geometric transformation <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the actions <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g}[\bm{a}]=[K_{g}[\bm{a}_{equ}],\bm{a}_{inv}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g}[\bm{a}]=[K_{g}[\bm{a}_{equ}],\bm{a}_{inv}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The SGDA leverages the inherent symmetry of multi-robot systems to expand the scope of given demonstrations. This module enables the demonstrations to cover a broader range of environmental states, effectively increasing the number of available demonstrations without the need for additional collection of costly demonstrations. Based on Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Symmetry-Guided MIRL Improvement) ‣ IV-C Symmetry-Guided MIRL ‣ IV Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, augmented demonstrations through SGDA can recover a more accurate reward function, thus improving the sample efficiency of MIRL algorithms.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Symmetry-Guided Framework.</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span>: Number of agents <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, expert demonstrations <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, geometric transformations <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize</span>: Generator network <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, discriminators <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\bm{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\bm{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
               Perform data augmentation on <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}\leftarrow\tau_{E}\cup g\tau_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}\leftarrow\tau_{E}\cup g\tau_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">repeat</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                Sample generator trajectories <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                Sample state-action pairs <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                 Perform data augmentation on <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                 Update <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In V-B Symmetry-Aware Discriminator ‣ V Symmetry-Guided Framework ‣ Symmetry-Guided Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                 Update <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the feedback of <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\omega_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\omega_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> by MAPPO.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                
                <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">until</span> Convergence

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
                
               <span id="alg1.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output</span>: Learned policy <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08257v1/res_rendezvous.svg" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/366;" width="476" height="366"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Rendezvous.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08257v1/res_pursuit.svg" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/354;" width="476" height="354"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pursuit.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08257v1/res_vicsek.svg" id="S5.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/361;" width="476" height="361"></object>
